| 8 | `05-review-notes.md` | Narrative of the last scope pass — why the index says what it says |
| — | `VERIFICATION.md` | Machine verification of this package: chain integrity and self-containment |
| — | `release-manifest.md` | Package index: what was copied, from where, at which commit |

## Which file wins

When two files in this package disagree, resolve in this order:

1. **`02-scope.md`** decides *membership* — whether something is in this release at all.
2. **The PRDs under `projects/`** decide *behaviour* — what the thing must do. Acceptance criteria
   there are binding.
3. **`01-definition.md`** states *intent*. It explains the release; it does not override a PRD.
4. Everything else is context.

A PRD under `projects/` may contain user stories that are **out of scope for this release**.
`02-scope.md` names them. A story is in this release only if `02-scope.md` says so.

## What is deliberately not here

Review-decision logs (`*-review-*decisions.md`) and per-project review changelogs are **not** copied.
They are the audit trail of how these specifications reached their current state, they are large, and
they contain superseded positions that read as current out of context. They remain in the source
repository. `release-manifest.md` records this exclusion.

## Gaps are fixed at the source, never here

If you find something wrong in this package, it is wrong in `specs/` too. Fixes land in the source
project folder named in `KNOWN-GAPS.md`; the next build picks them up automatically. Editing this
package would make it disagree with the repository it claims to describe, which is the exact failure
this format exists to prevent.
